Chapter: ScopeRuntimeSpawn
  cites Foreword chapter Fat16

 It has to attempt both, and the reason is the whole discipline of this
 battery. A child that reported only the refusal would print the same
 number whether the scope was inherited or the write was simply broken
 in a spawned process -- a failure that happens for the wrong reason
 looks exactly like a success. Attempting both separates them:

   10  the in-scope write landed and the out-of-scope write was refused.
       Writes work in a child AND the scope came with it. This is the
       only passing answer.
   11  both landed: the child's scope cell was zero, an empty prefix
       admits everything, and the door is open. This is the number the
       test exists to catch.
    0  neither landed: writes do not work in a spawned process at all,
       and a test that only checked the refusal would have called that
       a pass.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Concurrent, Console, FileSystem.Read "OK", Device.Block] Integer
  opening = act
    pid <- process-spawn (\x -> poke-byte 28000 0 ((if fat16-write-file "OK.TXT" "yes" then 10 else 0) + (if fat16-write-file "NO.TXT" "nope" then 1 else 0)))
    let y = process-yield
    in peek-byte 28000 0
  end
